<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Seeb</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1920">1920</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Agreement of Seeb</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of Seeb was an agreement concluded in 1920, through British mediation, between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscat">Muscat</a> and the tribal leaders of the Ibadi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imamate">Imamate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oman">Oman</a> in the interior. It ended the fighting that had begun with the imamate's rising in 1913 and left the interior largely self-governing. Its meaning was disputed in the 1950s, when the sultan reasserted control of the interior.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-seeb.jpg" alt="Treaty of Seeb" width="320" height="323" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: British National Archive · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1920_Treaty_of_Seeb.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imamate">Imamate</a> had appeared in cycles in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oman">Oman</a> for more than 1,200 years. British influence in the coastal Sultanate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscat">Muscat</a> revived its cause in the interior, and Imam <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salim-ibn-rashid-al-kharusi">Salim ibn Rashid al-Kharusi</a> led a rebellion against Muscat among the Ibadi tribes of the interior from 1913 to 1920. Both states were Ibadi, but the Imamate opposed foreign interference in internal affairs and held that the ruler should be elected. The settlement was reached on 25 September 1920 between Sultan Taimur bin Feisal and Isa bin Salih al-Harthi, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinawi">Hinawi</a> leader, who signed for the tribes, with the British Political Agent Ronald Wingate as mediator. It takes its name from the coastal town of Seeb.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>The text set out four conditions for each side. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a>'s government was to take no more than 5 percent on goods brought from the interior to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscat">Muscat</a>, Matrah, Sur and the other coast towns, to give all Omanis safety and freedom there and lift restrictions on entry and exit, and not to shelter fugitives from Omani justice or interfere in the interior's internal affairs. The tribes and shaikhs in turn were to keep peace with the Sultan, not attack the coast towns or meddle in his government, let lawful travellers and trade pass freely, expel criminals who fled to them, and hear merchants' claims according to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharia">sharia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imamate">Imamate</a> made its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizwa">Nizwa</a>, and relations stayed largely peaceful until 1945, when news of Imam <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-al-khalili">Muhammad bin Abdullah al-Khalili</a>'s illness led Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/said-bin-taimur">Said bin Taimur</a> to tell the British he wished to occupy the interior after the Imam's death. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghalib-alhinai">Ghalib Alhinai</a> was elected Imam in 1954.</p><p>On 25 October 1954 the Muscat Infantry under Lieutenant Colonel Coriat entered Fahud, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imamate">Imamate</a> held to be a breach of the treaty, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jebel-akhdar-war">Jebel Akhdar War</a> followed. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizwa">Nizwa</a> in 1955; Saudi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> backed the Imamate, and Talib bin Ali Alhinai revived the rebellion. After British intervention, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-air-force">Royal Air Force</a> and the Special Air Service, the war ended in 1959 with the rebels defeated and their leaders exiled to Saudi Arabia.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Treaty of Seeb” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Treaty of Seeb</b> of 1920 gave the interior wide autonomy.</blockquote><a href="/state/imamate-of-oman">Imamate of Oman</a></li><li><blockquote>The interior Ibadi tribes resented the coastal sultans; Azzan ibn Qais briefly held Muscat as imam in 1868-1871, and the Imamate of Oman rose in 1913, kept at bay by the <b>Treaty of Seeb</b> in 1920.</blockquote><a href="/state/sultanate-of-muscat-and-oman">Sultanate of Muscat and Oman</a></li></ul></section>
